/// \defgroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd Named UI callbacks, the WINCLASS and DRAWCALLBACK fields, and the replay-control helpers
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief Callbacks that UI data reaches by name (an Apt stats-tab command,
///        the IME candidate window's draw and system callbacks), the movie
///        window's last-frame callback (bound by address, it calls an
///        ActionScript function the data names), the two .wnd field parsers
///        that resolve a window class and a draw callback by name, the %Int
///        scan helper of the .wnd parsers, and the replay-control window
///        helpers.
///
/// Much of the UI never calls these functions directly. Most of them are
/// named by data, and the game resolves the name when the data is loaded
/// or run. This page covers two of those naming systems. It also covers
/// the Apt movie window's last-frame callback, which no data names, and
/// some helpers that sit beside them.
///
/// \par Apt callbacks
/// An Apt screen's controller can register callbacks under strings, and
/// the movie's script then calls them by string (see
/// \ref sub_gui_apt_screens). On this page only one function is reached
/// that way:
/// - AptOnline::Stats registers its own AptOnline::Stats::CurrentTab under
///   the string "AptOnline::Stats::CurrentTab". The online statistics movie
///   calls it with a tab name.
///
/// The other Apt callback on this page is bound by address, not by name:
/// - The Apt movie window's set-up code reads a query string of parameters
///   that come from the Apt data: _MovieName, _Loop, _UseAlpha,
///   _HoldLastFrame and _CallOnLastFrame. Every parameter after _MovieName
///   is read only when _MovieName is present. If _MovieName is present and
///   _CallOnLastFrame is too, the set-up code keeps the _CallOnLastFrame
///   value, an ActionScript function name, on the window. It then wraps
///   the address of _CallOnLastFrame() as the movie's last-frame callback
///   and sends it to the window in a system message. The set-up code
///   registers no string for _CallOnLastFrame(); it hands over only the
///   function's address. What the data names is the ActionScript function
///   that _CallOnLastFrame() calls. The parameter names are string
///   literals in the retail set-up code.
///
/// \par Function lexicon callbacks
/// The legacy window system (GameWindowManager) reads .wnd layout scripts,
/// whose fields name a window's callbacks as quoted strings. The field
/// parsers turn each name into a name key (TheNameKeyGenerator) and look
/// the key up in the function lexicon (TheFunctionLexicon, a
/// FunctionLexicon). The lexicon is a set of tables built into the game,
/// and each entry pairs a name with a function. FunctionLexicon::findFunction
/// searches one table, or every table in turn. The keyword table and the
/// loader that calls these parsers are described in
/// \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers. This page adds two parsers:
/// - parseDrawCallback() for the DRAWCALLBACK field, as in Zero Hour;
/// - parseWinClass() for the WINCLASS field. Zero Hour's keyword table has
///   no WINCLASS field. BFME 1 already has one, paired with a
///   near-identical parser, and BFME 2 keeps it.
///
/// The IME candidate window's IMECandidateMainDraw(),
/// IMECandidateTextAreaDraw() and IMECandidateWindowSystem() are lexicon
/// entries: retail's lexicon lists each one by name. A layout reaches them
/// through these parsers.
///
/// \par Helpers
/// scanInt() is one of the number-scanning helpers that the gadget-data
/// and rectangle parsers use. Its siblings include scanBool(), documented
/// with the other parsers, and scanShort(), which parseListboxData() and
/// the TEXTENTRYDATA parser use and which stores the value truncated to a
/// Short. showReplayControls(), hideReplayControls()
/// and toggleReplayControls() show or hide the replay-control window. They
/// are not bound by name. BFME 2's HideControlBar calls
/// hideReplayControls(). Zero Hour's ShowControlBar and ToggleControlBar
/// call the other two, but BFME 2's versions of those functions do not, and
/// no named BFME 2 caller of them has been found.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which callbacks a .wnd window uses (any name the lexicon
///   holds), the window class a WINCLASS field names, and, in Apt data, the
///   video an Apt movie window plays and the ActionScript function it calls
///   when the video ends (the _MovieName and _CallOnLastFrame parameters
///   above). The online statistics movie decides when to switch tabs, but
///   the tab names it may pass are fixed in code.
/// - Hard-coded: the lexicon's tables are built into the game, so a layout
///   can only name functions the executable already registers. A new
///   callback needs code. The three statistics tab names are compared in
///   code, case-sensitively. The IME candidate list's frame colour and
///   number-column layout are fixed in code (see
///   IMECandidateTextAreaDraw()).
/// - An unknown name is not an error. The lexicon lookup returns null.
///   Zero Hour's window creation then keeps the window's default callback.
///   An unknown window name given to AptPlayer::WinCreate means the window
///   is not created (see parseWinClass()). An unknown statistics tab name
///   is ignored.
/// - The bodies on this page work only on UI objects. The replay helpers
///   read the game mode but change no GameLogic state. A change here
///   therefore does not affect lockstep determinism, unless a script
///   function or screen routine that these callbacks start does.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Video: _CallOnLastFrame() is where a finished movie hands control back
///   to the Apt script. A replacement video player must still report the
///   last frame, or the script function the Apt data names for that moment
///   never runs. The decoder itself is not covered here.
/// - Input method: IMEManager::init builds the candidate list from
///   IMECandidateWindow.wnd and drives it from the Windows IMM API
///   (ImmCreateContext, ImmGetContext). The list is drawn by the engine's
///   own window system with the callbacks on this page, not by the
///   operating system. A port that uses the platform's text-input UI
///   instead can drop these callbacks. A port that keeps them must feed
///   the same candidate queries (IMEManager::getCandidateCount and its
///   siblings).
/// - Pixel layout: the candidate list uses fixed pixel spacing (a 10-pixel
///   number gutter, a line spacing variable that starts at 2 pixels, and
///   one-pixel borders). These do not scale with resolution.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers,
///      \ref grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw


/// \class AptOnline::Stats
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief Controller object of the online statistics Apt screen. It tracks
///        the selected tab (Tournament, OpenPlay or Strategic) and runs that
///        tab's routine when the movie switches tabs.
///
/// Like the other Apt screen controllers (see \ref sub_gui_apt_screens),
/// a factory function allocates and constructs it when its screen is
/// created. The constructor:
/// - starts on the first tab (Tournament);
/// - registers AptOnline::Stats::CurrentTab as an Apt command under the
///   string "AptOnline::Stats::CurrentTab", bound to this object, so the
///   movie's script can switch tabs;
/// - stores itself in a global pointer unless another instance is already
///   stored there.
///
/// The constructor's body shows all of this. The two base-class
/// views the constructor installs give the object an Apt screen-controller
/// part and an AptStats part. The constructor builds the AptStats part with
/// the object as its owner. That these views are base classes, not members,
/// is an inference from the two virtual table pointers. The class name
/// comes from the registered string. How long the object lives, and when
/// the global pointer is cleared, has not been traced.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The tab names are fixed in code, and each tab has its own native
/// routine. The movie can only switch between those three tabs. A
/// fourth tab, or a renamed one, needs code.
///
/// \see AptOnline::Stats::CurrentTab


/// \fn void AptOnline::Stats::CurrentTab(const char *tab)
/// \brief Apt command callback: switches the online statistics screen to
///        the named tab and runs that tab's routine.
///
/// The online statistics movie calls this through the command string the
/// constructor registers (see AptOnline::Stats). That registration is the
/// function's only reference in retail.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Compares the argument with "Tournament", "OpenPlay" and "Strategic",
///   in that order, case-sensitively, and stores the matching tab index.
/// - Any other string returns at once. The stored tab and the screen do
///   not change.
/// - After a match it calls a member routine of the screen. That routine
///   calls a helper on the AptStats part and a helper on another object
///   the screen holds. It then calls one of three per-tab routines, chosen
///   by the new tab index, and finishes with a call on the AptStats part.
///   This page does not describe what each tab shows.
///
/// \param tab  Tab name passed by the movie's script; must not be null.
///
/// \see AptOnline::Stats


/// \file Apt/AptOnlineStatsCallbacks.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief AptOnline::Stats::CurrentTab, the online statistics screen's
///        tab-switch command.


/// \file Apt/AptBinkMovieCallbacks.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief _CallOnLastFrame(), the Apt movie window's last-frame callback.


/// \fn void _CallOnLastFrame(GameWindow *window)
/// \brief Runs the ActionScript function that the Apt data named in a movie
///        window's _CallOnLastFrame parameter, on the movie level that
///        hosts the window.
///
/// The movie window's set-up code attaches this function as the window's
/// last-frame callback, and only when the window's parameters include both
/// _MovieName and _CallOnLastFrame (see the group description). The project
/// names this function after that parameter. Retail binds it by address,
/// so it carries no name of its own. Being bound there is the only
/// reference to it.
/// The function is therefore meant to run when the window's movie reaches
/// its last frame. The code that makes the call at that point has not been
/// traced here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns at once for a null window. The WorldBuilder build's twin also
///   asserts first ("Expecting a param here.") and then returns.
/// - Gets the window's Apt movie level from AptPlayer::GetLevelIndex. That
///   function sends a query message up the window's parent chain, and the
///   first window that answers supplies the level.
/// - Calls the ActionScript function whose name the set-up code stored on
///   the window, at the path "/_level<n>" of that level. It passes one
///   string argument, which is a second string the window holds. The
///   project's ledger notes read it as the window's name; this page has not
///   confirmed that independently. The call helper ignores levels
///   of 14 and above. GetLevelIndex returns 14 when no parent answers, so a
///   movie window outside any Apt movie calls nothing.
/// - The function's result is not used.
///
/// \param window  The movie window whose movie ended; null is allowed and
///                ignored.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The function to call is plain Apt data. When a movie window's
/// parameters set _MovieName and also set _CallOnLastFrame to an
/// ActionScript function name, that function is called in the window's own
/// movie level, with one string argument, when the video ends.
/// _CallOnLastFrame has an effect only together with _MovieName: without
/// _MovieName it is never read.
/// The parameters form a query string of key=value pairs separated by
/// ampersands. The other parameters the set-up code reads are _MovieName
/// (the video; without it the window gets no movie at all), _Loop,
/// _UseAlpha and _HoldLastFrame. Each of the three flags is set when its
/// value contains a lowercase t anywhere, so "true" sets it and "TRUE" does
/// not.
///
/// \warning A missing flag parameter is not read as false. The parameter
/// reader leaves its output string unchanged when a key is absent, and the
/// set-up code reuses one string for every parameter. So it tests the last
/// value it read: a missing _Loop tests the movie name for a t. Give all
/// three flags explicitly, for example _Loop=false.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is the hand-off from video playback back to the UI script. A
/// replacement player must still call the window's last-frame callback.
///
/// \see AptPlayer::GetLevelIndex


/// \file GUICallbacks/IMECandidate.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief The IME candidate window's draw and system callbacks, and the
///        one display string they share.


/// \fn void IMECandidateMainDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for the IME candidate window's frame: a bordered,
///        filled background in the colours for the window's state.
///
/// A layout reaches this function by name through parseDrawCallback(), and
/// retail's function lexicon lists it. IMEManager::init creates the
/// candidate window from IMECandidateWindow.wnd. This page has not checked
/// which windows of that layout name this callback.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The state picks the first draw-data slot's colour and border colour:
///   disabled when the window is not enabled, else highlighted when the
///   instance's highlighted bit is set, else enabled.
/// - When the border colour is defined, draws a one-pixel outline around
///   the whole window. When the fill colour is defined, fills the window
///   one pixel inside that outline.
/// - BFME 2's body has the same logic as Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param window    The candidate window being drawn.
/// \param instData  Its instance data, which supplies the state bits.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The colours come from the layout's draw data. Leaving a colour undefined
/// skips that part.
///
/// \see IMECandidateTextAreaDraw(), IMECandidateWindowSystem()


/// \fn void IMECandidateTextAreaDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for the IME candidate list: a framed black panel
///        holding one numbered row per candidate on the current page, with
///        the selected candidate in the highlight colour.
///
/// Its name, and its use of the window's user data as the IME manager, fit
/// the TextArea child of IMECandidateWindow.wnd. IMEManager::init finds that
/// window and sets its user data to the IME manager (TheIMEManager). This
/// page has not checked the layout itself. A layout reaches the callback by
/// name through parseDrawCallback(), and retail's function lexicon lists
/// it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Text colours: a disabled window draws every row in the disabled text
///   colour. Otherwise the selected row uses the highlight text colour and
///   the other rows use the enabled text colour. Highlighting the window
///   itself does not change this.
/// - Always draws a one-pixel frame in a fixed green, then fills the inside
///   with black.
/// - Stops after the frame when no display string exists yet (see
///   IMECandidateWindowSystem()) or when the window has no user data.
/// - Takes the window's font for the shared display string. The row height
///   is the font height plus IMECandidateWindowLineSpacing, a global that
///   starts at 2 and that IMEManager::resizeCandidateWindow also uses to
///   size the window. Text is clipped to the window rectangle.
/// - Asks the IME manager for the page start, candidate count, page size,
///   selected index and index base. It draws one row for each candidate
///   from the page start, up to the page size or the end of the list.
/// - Every row has a number column of the same width: the width of the
///   text "00:" plus a 10-pixel gutter from the left edge. Each row number
///   ("<n>:", counted from the manager's index base) is right-aligned to
///   that column, and the candidate text starts just after it.
/// - Before each row it sets the display string's text colour once
///   (setTextColor), with black as the second colour, and each of the
///   row's two draw calls (the number, then the candidate) passes 1, 1 as
///   its last two arguments. BFME 1's declaration names these a
///   drop-shadow colour and offset, so the text gets a one-pixel black
///   shadow.
///
/// \param window    The candidate text-area window; its user data must be
///                  the IME manager or null.
/// \param instData  Its instance data, which supplies the state bits.
///
/// \note Zero Hour draws only a black outline and no fill, and passes the
/// row colours to the draw call. BFME 1 has the same frame-and-fill shape
/// as BFME 2 but with an orange frame. BFME 2's frame is green. The frame,
/// fill and shadow colours ignore the layout's draw data.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Only the text colours (disabled, enabled and highlight) and the font come
/// from the layout. To change the frame colour or the column layout, the
/// code must change.
///
/// \see IMECandidateMainDraw(), IMECandidateWindowSystem()


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType IMECandidateWindowSystem(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData data1, WindowMsgData data2)
/// \brief System callback for the IME candidate window. It creates the
///        candidate list's display string when the window is created and
///        frees it when the window is destroyed.
///
/// A layout reaches this function by name through the SYSTEMCALLBACK
/// field's parser, and retail's function lexicon lists it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - On the create message, gets a new display string from
///   TheDisplayStringManager, unless the file already has one.
/// - On the destroy message, returns the display string to the manager
///   and clears the file's pointer.
/// - Returns MSG_HANDLED for those two messages and MSG_IGNORED for all
///   others.
/// - There is one display string for the whole file. Every window that
///   uses this callback shares it, and destroying any of them frees it for
///   all. IMECandidateTextAreaDraw() then draws only its frame until a
///   window is created again. The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param window  The window receiving the message (not used).
/// \param msg     The system message.
/// \param data1   First message argument (not used).
/// \param data2   Second message argument (not used).
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for create and destroy, otherwise MSG_IGNORED.
///
/// \see IMECandidateTextAreaDraw()


/// \file GUI/GameWindowManagerScript_parseWinClass.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief parseWinClass(), the parser for the WINCLASS field of a .wnd
///        window entry.


/// \fn static Bool parseWinClass(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Reads the quoted name in a WINCLASS field and looks it up in the
///        function lexicon. It keeps both the name and the function found
///        for the window being built.
///
/// Zero Hour's keyword table has no WINCLASS field. BFME 1 already has one,
/// paired with a near-identical parser, and BFME 2 keeps it: BFME 2
/// retail's table pairs WINCLASS with this parser. BFME 1's parser makes
/// the same quote scan and strtok call, stores the name in the same string
/// the SYSTEMCALLBACK parser uses, and passes the name's key to a
/// function-lexicon lookup with the same arguments (Open-BFME-1's identity
/// evidence for its WINCLASS parser records the field-table pairing). The
/// window-entry loader calls
/// it when a window entry has a WINCLASS line (see
/// \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Skips to the first double quote in the value and takes the text up to
///   the next one as the name, using strtok.
/// - Stores the name in a file-level string, makes a name key from it and
///   looks the key up in the lexicon. The search covers one table and then
///   a second, through the same lookup helper that AptPlayer::WinCreate
///   uses. The function found, or null, goes into a file-level slot for the
///   window entry being parsed.
/// - Always returns true. An unknown name is not an error.
///
/// What a WINCLASS entry is has not been fully traced. AptPlayer::WinCreate
/// looks up a window's name in the same two tables and puts the result into
/// the creation record it gives the window manager. It refuses to create
/// the window when the name is not found. This suggests that a WINCLASS
/// name selects the function that builds the window's class. That reading
/// is an inference, and the loader's use of the stored slot is described
/// only by its contract.
///
/// \param token     The keyword (not used).
/// \param instData  The window's instance data (not used).
/// \param buffer    The field's value text. It must contain a double
///                  quote, and strtok modifies it.
/// \param data      Gadget data pointer (not used).
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \warning Retail stores the name in the same file-level string the
/// SYSTEMCALLBACK parser uses. Whichever of the two fields comes last in an
/// entry decides that saved name. Window creation (createWindow) copies the
/// name into the window's edit data, when the window has edit data, as its
/// system-callback name. The function pointers are kept in separate slots.
/// The value is not bounds-checked: a value with no double quote makes the
/// scan run past the buffer.
///
/// \see parseDrawCallback(), AptPlayer::WinCreate, FunctionLexicon::findFunction


/// \file GUI/GameWindowManagerScript_parseDrawCallback.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief parseDrawCallback(), the parser for the DRAWCALLBACK field of a
///        .wnd window entry.


/// \fn static Bool parseDrawCallback(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Reads the quoted draw-function name in a DRAWCALLBACK field and
///        resolves it through the function lexicon for the window being
///        built.
///
/// Retail's .wnd keyword table pairs DRAWCALLBACK with this parser. Names
/// it can resolve include IMECandidateMainDraw() and
/// IMECandidateTextAreaDraw().
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Skips to the first double quote and takes the text up to the next one
///   as the name, using strtok.
/// - Stores the name in the file-level draw-callback name string. Window
///   creation (createWindow) copies that string into the window's edit
///   data, when the window has edit data.
/// - Makes a name key and looks it up in the lexicon, one table first and
///   then a second. This is the same two-step search that Zero Hour's
///   FunctionLexicon::gameWinDrawFunc makes: device-specific draw functions
///   first, then device-independent ones. That BFME 2's table numbers keep
///   Zero Hour's meaning is an inference from that shape.
/// - The function found, or null, goes into the file-level draw-callback
///   slot. Always returns true.
///
/// Zero Hour: the window parser clears these slots at the start of each
/// window entry and passes them to createWindow when the window is
/// created. BFME 2's createWindow takes a creation record instead. The
/// loader that fills that record is described only by its contract.
///
/// \param token     The keyword (not used).
/// \param instData  The window's instance data (not used).
/// \param buffer    The field's value text. It must contain a double
///                  quote, and strtok modifies it.
/// \param data      Gadget data pointer (not used).
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's. Only the lexicon lookup goes through
/// BFME 2's helper.
/// \warning A value with no double quote makes the scan run past the
/// buffer.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Zero Hour searches the device-specific table first, so its W3D device
/// layer can register its own draw functions under names that layouts use.
/// If BFME 2's table order means the same (see above), a new renderer can
/// take over a layout's draw callbacks the same way, without editing the
/// layouts.
///
/// \see parseWinClass(), FunctionLexicon::findFunction


/// \file GUI/GameWindowManagerScript_scanInt.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief scanInt(), the %Int scan helper of the .wnd parsers.


/// \fn Int scanInt(const char *source, Int &val)
/// \brief Reads a decimal integer from text into an %Int.
///
/// A thin wrapper over sscanf with the %d format, which writes straight
/// into the caller's variable. Retail callers include parseScreenRect(),
/// parseSliderData(), parseRadioButtonData(), parseTooltipDelay(),
/// parseListboxData(), parseComboBoxData(), parseTabControlData(),
/// parseFont(), parseTextColor() and parseDrawData().
///
/// \param source  Text to scan; must not be null.
/// \param val     [out] The value read. It is left unchanged when the text
///                is not a number.
/// \return sscanf's result: 1 when a number was read.
///
/// \note Zero Hour has separate %Int and %UnsignedInt versions with the same
/// body. The colour parsers (parseTextColor(), parseDrawData()) use the
/// unsigned one. In BFME 2's retail code they reach this body, which fits
/// the folding noted at scanBool().
///
/// \see scanBool()


/// \file GameClient/ReplayControls.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd
/// \brief showReplayControls(), hideReplayControls() and
///        toggleReplayControls(), the replay-control window helpers.


/// \fn void showReplayControls(void)
/// \brief Shows the replay-control window during a replay and hides it
///        otherwise.
///
/// All three helpers act on one global window pointer, the replay-control
/// window, and do nothing while it is null. Zero Hour: InGameUI keeps that
/// window, InGameUI::createReplayControl loads it from ReplayControl.wnd,
/// and ShowControlBar calls this function. BFME 2's ShowControlBar does
/// not, and no named BFME 2 caller of this function has been found. This
/// page has not traced where BFME 2 creates the window.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Hides the window unless the game logic (TheGameLogic) reports a replay
/// game. BFME 2 makes that test by comparing the game logic's mode with the
/// value Zero Hour's GameLogic::isInReplayGame tests for. Zero Hour has the
/// same branches.
///
/// \see hideReplayControls(), toggleReplayControls()


/// \fn void hideReplayControls(void)
/// \brief Hides the replay-control window.
///
/// BFME 2's HideControlBar calls this, as Zero Hour's does. Does nothing
/// while there is no replay-control window.
///
/// \see showReplayControls(), toggleReplayControls()


/// \fn void toggleReplayControls(void)
/// \brief Shows the replay-control window if a replay is running and the
///        window is hidden, and hides it in every other case.
///
/// Zero Hour: ToggleControlBar calls this. BFME 2's ToggleControlBar does
/// not, and no named BFME 2 caller of this function has been found. Does
/// nothing while there is no replay-control window.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Shows the window only when the game logic reports a replay game and
/// the window is currently hidden. Otherwise it hides the window. Outside a
/// replay, the toggle can therefore only hide the window. The branches are
/// Zero Hour's.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The replay-control window's contents come from its layout. Whether it
/// may be shown is fixed in code: only in a replay game.
///
/// \see showReplayControls(), hideReplayControls()
